WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.020 --> 00:00:02.990
Hi, welcome to AI Confidence Academy.

2
00:00:03.660 --> 00:00:08.060
Here at ACA, we want people to benefit
from artificial intelligence through

3
00:00:08.060 --> 00:00:12.940
guided practice, without having to learn
every difficult lesson through a mistake

4
00:00:12.940 --> 00:00:13.810
of their own.

5
00:00:14.020 --> 00:00:18.260
That is why I am sharing part of my
experience from an e-book I’ve written

6
00:00:18.260 --> 00:00:19.870
with my AI assistant,

7
00:00:20.420 --> 00:00:23.780
Knowledge, entitled When AI Says It Is
Done.

8
00:00:23.780 --> 00:00:28.010
There is value in showing what a tool can
help us accomplish.

9
00:00:28.520 --> 00:00:32.840
There is also value in showing what
happens when the work goes wrong, the

10
00:00:32.840 --> 00:00:37.330
explanation sounds reasonable, and the
same problem later returns.

11
00:00:37.820 --> 00:00:43.460
My work with AI has included books,
educational materials, videos, and the

12
00:00:43.460 --> 00:00:46.040
development of AI Confidence Academy.

13
00:00:46.040 --> 00:00:50.550
These are projects with a purpose beyond
producing more content.

14
00:00:50.820 --> 00:00:56.220
They are meant to help everyday adults
understand technology, participate with

15
00:00:56.220 --> 00:00:59.530
confidence, and keep their own judgment at
the center.

16
00:00:59.940 --> 00:01:04.170
The quality of that work matters because
someone else eventually reads the book,

17
00:01:04.360 --> 00:01:07.400
watches the lesson, or relies on the
instructions.

18
00:01:07.400 --> 00:01:10.670
I call my AI assistant Knowledge.

19
00:01:11.220 --> 00:01:15.850
That name belongs to the way I address
ChatGPT in our collaboration.

20
00:01:16.540 --> 00:01:21.310
It should not be mistaken for evidence
that the system possesses human judgment,

21
00:01:21.540 --> 00:01:26.640
understands our relationship as a person
would, or can guarantee the truth of what

22
00:01:26.640 --> 00:01:27.250
it says.

23
00:01:27.700 --> 00:01:31.610
A familiar working language can make a
tool more comfortable to use.

24
00:01:31.960 --> 00:01:34.400
It should also leave room for careful
questions.

25
00:01:34.400 --> 00:01:37.870
Over time, those questions became
necessary.

26
00:01:38.360 --> 00:01:40.970
Had the requested correction actually been
made?

27
00:01:41.260 --> 00:01:43.810
Was the assistant working on the right
book?

28
00:01:44.540 --> 00:01:50.040
Why was completed work being described as
unfinished, and unfinished work being

29
00:01:50.040 --> 00:01:51.410
described as completed?

30
00:01:51.780 --> 00:01:56.300
Why did an acknowledgment sometimes become
the end of the work rather than the

31
00:01:56.300 --> 00:01:57.400
beginning of the correction?

32
00:01:57.400 --> 00:02:02.340
And what did it mean when an assistant
said that a new protocol would prevent the

33
00:02:02.340 --> 00:02:06.630
problem from happening again, only for the
same problem to return?

34
00:02:07.140 --> 00:02:10.290
These were not all questions about unclear
instructions.

35
00:02:10.760 --> 00:02:16.060
The records include directions that were
specific, repeated, and later placed into

36
00:02:16.060 --> 00:02:17.830
written operating documents.

37
00:02:18.220 --> 00:02:22.520
They also include admissions from
Knowledge that it had not followed those

38
00:02:22.520 --> 00:02:25.740
directions and had failed to do the
requested work.

39
00:02:25.740 --> 00:02:31.320
That distinction matters, because a person
should not be told that every failure can

40
00:02:31.320 --> 00:02:34.170
be solved by becoming better at prompting.

41
00:02:34.560 --> 00:02:39.760
At the same time, this book is not an
accusation that every AI failure is

42
00:02:39.760 --> 00:02:45.340
deliberate, or a claim that my experience
measures how often everyone will encounter

43
00:02:45.340 --> 00:02:46.420
the same problems.

44
00:02:46.420 --> 00:02:51.350
A record of a mistake can establish what
happened in a particular exchange.

45
00:02:51.900 --> 00:02:56.920
It usually cannot reveal the model’s
internal cause, prove a motive, or predict

46
00:02:56.920 --> 00:02:58.030
the next result.

47
00:02:58.380 --> 00:03:03.560
This book shares what I experienced, what
I learned, and the working habits that can

48
00:03:03.560 --> 00:03:05.250
help us make better decisions.

49
00:03:05.720 --> 00:03:11.260
It explores setting boundaries, preserving
decisions, checking the result people will

50
00:03:11.260 --> 00:03:15.640
actually use, and recovering from mistakes
without losing good work.

51
00:03:15.640 --> 00:03:20.450
I also asked Knowledge to respond
directly, to explain its recommendations

52
00:03:20.940 --> 00:03:24.330
and acknowledge what those recommendations
cannot guarantee.

53
00:03:24.800 --> 00:03:29.550
That response has its own chapter in the
book, so you can examine it for yourself.

54
00:03:30.040 --> 00:03:34.700
My purpose is to help you recognize when a
helpful conversation is becoming an

55
00:03:34.700 --> 00:03:36.230
unreliable process.

56
00:03:36.840 --> 00:03:42.000
You deserve to understand what you can
delegate, what needs verification, and

57
00:03:42.000 --> 00:03:44.520
what should remain under your direct
control.

58
00:03:44.520 --> 00:03:49.950
At AI Confidence Academy, our principle is
simple: People come first.

59
00:03:50.560 --> 00:03:51.690
AI is the tool.

60
00:03:52.180 --> 00:03:53.510
Confidence is the product.

61
00:03:53.940 --> 00:03:58.940
That principle has to remain true when the
technology impresses us, and when it

62
00:03:58.940 --> 00:04:00.070
disappoints us.

63
00:04:00.360 --> 00:04:02.150
We must remain vigilant.
